      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "currencyUSD",
          "color": {
            "mode": "palette-classic"
          },
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "fillOpacity": 0,
            "stacking": {
              "mode": "none",
              "group": "A"
            }
          }
        },
        "overrides": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "type": "logs",
      "title": "Daemon log lines - JSON, with trace_id where a span was in scope",
      "description": "One object per line from the API, orchestrator and investigator (T6.6 piece 4). `trace_id` on a line is a derived field on the Loki datasource and links to the trace in Tempo. Empty on a development machine, where the daemons run on the host and promtail reads containers; filled on the VM.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "loki",
        "uid": "loki"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 10,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 20
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "loki",
            "uid": "loki"
          },
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "{service=~\".*(faultline|orchestrator|executor).*\"} | json | component =~ \"api|orchestrator|investigator\""
        }
      ],
      "options": {
        "showTime": true,
        "wrapLogMessage": true,
        "enableLogDetails": true,
        "sortOrder": "Descending",
        "dedupStrategy": "none",
        "prettifyLogMessage": false,
        "showLabels": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Recent investigation traces - service faultline",
      "description": "Tempo search for the agent's own spans: one root `incident` per run - opened by the runner before triage, so the triage judgement's model call is inside it rather than a trace of its own (first-trace note, addendum 2) - with `investigation` beneath it and `model.call`, `tool.call`, `backend.get` beneath that. A run triage declines has the root and the triage call and no `investigation`, and this panel filters on `investigation`, so it lists runs that investigated. Click a trace id to open the waterfall. Grafana attaches the time picker's range to trace lookups, so a run older than the range reads as not found; search over a wide range was once observed to miss a block a narrow range found, and recovered on its own (addendum 2).",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "tempo",
        "uid": "tempo"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 10,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 20
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "tempo",
            "uid": "tempo"
          },
          "refId": "A",
          "queryType": "nativeSearch",
          "serviceName": "faultline",
          "spanName": "investigation",
          "limit": 20
        }
      ],
      "options": {
        "showHeader": true
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {},
        "overrides": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Why the Prometheus panels may be empty",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 5,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 30
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**`/metrics` is served and nothing scrapes it yet.** The scrape job belongs in `compose/prometheus/prometheus-config.yaml`, which is inside `observability_digest`, and the harness reads that Prometheus' target set as a measurement (`max(count_over_time(up[...]))` counts scrapes). A digest-locked change queues rather than lands: **Q73** in `docs/QUEUE.md`. Until it lands, `curl -s <api>/metrics` reads the same numbers directly. The Loki and Tempo panels do not depend on it."
      }
    }
  ]
}
